"According to the passage, why can the four named kinds of qi, taken separately, be misleading?": "972106d96fb0",
"An earlier lesson in this unit named four distinct kinds of qi — source, gathering, nutritive, and defensive — each with its own origin and job. Taken separately, this can make qi sound like four different substances working side by side rather than one coherent whole. Chinese medicine has a term for the combined, functioning result: \"true qi,\" the actual qi circulating through a living body once source qi has been activated by gathering qi and distributed as nutritive and defensive qi together.": "23053ec8738a",
"An earlier lesson in this unit named four distinct kinds of qi — source, gathering, nutritive, and defensive — each with its own origin and job. Taken separately, this can make qi sound like four different substances working side by side rather than one coherent whole. Chinese medicine has a term for the combined, functioning result: \"true qi,\" the actual qi circulating through a living body once source qi has been activated by gathering qi and distributed as nutritive and defensive qi together. The distinction matters because the four named varieties are, in a strict sense, components and precursors rather than the finished product doing the day-to-day work. Source qi sits in reserve; gathering qi is freshly produced but not yet distributed; nutritive and defensive qi are already circulating, but describing them separately obscures that a living body experiences them as a single, integrated force rather than four separate streams running in parallel. True qi is, in effect, this course's answer to a question a careful reader might have been holding since its first lesson: if qi is one concept with four sources and functions, what do you call it once it's actually working in a real, living person? The answer is that \"true qi\" was never a fifth, separate category — it's simply the name for the other four once they've stopped being distinguished from each other and are doing what qi actually does.": "addff1676371",
